b.Plugin vs Theme Function
Recommended Approach- PLUGIN 
1.Long-Term Maintenance & Theme Independence 
Business Need - The bakery may update their theme for seasonal redesigns or rebranding.
Plugin Benefits- Functionality remains intact during theme changes, clear separation of concerns (features vs. design).
Theme Function Risks- Moving to a new theme could break weekly specials logic, requires re-implementation if changing themes.
Impact- Prevents disruption to promotions during holiday theme updates (e.g., Christmas redesign).

2. Client Needs (Non-Technical Staff & Future Scalability)
Business Need- Staff must manage specials without developer help.
Plugin Benefits-Can include a custom admin UI (e.g. simplified metaboxes), easily extendable, e.g. adding POS integrations later.
Theme Function Limitations- Tightly coupled with theme files, harder to modify independently.
Impact- Staff can update specials without fear of breaking the website

3. Security & Updates
Business Need- Safe, stable updates without conflicts.
Plugin Benefits- Version-controlled separately from theme, security patches can be applied without touching theme files.
Theme Function Risks-Mixing business logic with presentation increases vulnerability surface.
Impact-If a POS API update is needed, it can be patched without waiting for a theme update.

4. Reusability & Portability
Business Need- Potential for multi-location expansion.
Plugin Benefits-Can be reused across multiple sites, easily disabled if feature is deprecated.
Theme Function Drawbacks- Locked into a single theme’s ecosystem.
Impact- If the bakery opens a second location, the plugin can be installed without rebuilding functionality.

